{"name":"Hedgerow Plumage","hex":"#c6795f","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hedgerow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A row of closely planted bushes or trees forming a hedge."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hedgerow"},{"term":"plumage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The feathers covering the body of a bird collectively.","Feathers used ornamentally; feathering;  a bunch or tuft of such feathers; a plume."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plumage"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hedge","blocks":[{"text":"A hedge or hedgerow is a line of shrubs and sometimes trees, planted and trained to form a barrier or to mark the boundary of an area, such as between neighbouring properties. Hedges that are used to separate a road from adjoining fields or one field from another, and are of sufficient age to incorporate larger trees, are known as hedgerows. Often they serve as windbreaks to improve conditions for the adjacent crops, as in bocage country. When clipped and maintained, hedges are also a simple form of topiary. Hedges and Hedgerows are usually no wider than about three feet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Rotbuchenhecke_in_der_Eifel.jpg/330px-Rotbuchenhecke_in_der_Eifel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedge","wikidata":"Q235779"},{"title":"Plumage","blocks":[{"text":"Plumage, or feathering, is a layer of feathers that covers a bird and the pattern, colour, and arrangement of those feathers. The pattern and colours of plumage differ between species and subspecies and may vary with sex and age classes. Within a few species, there can be different colour morphs. The placement of feathers on a bird is not haphazard but rather emerges in organised, overlapping rows and groups, and these feather tracts are known by standard names."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Plumage_house_sparrow.jpg/330px-Plumage_house_sparrow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plumage","wikidata":"Q3303665"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Rotbuchenhecke_in_der_Eifel.jpg/500px-Rotbuchenhecke_in_der_Eifel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Rotbuchenhecke_in_der_Eifel.jpg/500px-Rotbuchenhecke_in_der_Eifel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A typical clipped European beech hedge in the Eifel, Germany","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARotbuchenhecke_in_der_Eifel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Senecio_angulatus_shrub_form.jpg/500px-Senecio_angulatus_shrub_form.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Senecio_angulatus_shrub_form.jpg/500px-Senecio_angulatus_shrub_form.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A round hedge of creeping groundsel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASenecio_angulatus_shrub_form.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Littlestane_march_dyke.JPG/500px-Littlestane_march_dyke.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Littlestane_march_dyke.JPG/1280px-Littlestane_march_dyke.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Littlestane_march_dyke.JPG/500px-Littlestane_march_dyke.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A typical old Scottish march dyke, but without boundary trees","link":